  "provider_query": "The first YouTube video, Me at the zoo",
  "answer": "**“Me at the zoo”** is widely recognized as the first video uploaded to YouTube.\n\n1. **Original video** — 19-second clip by YouTube co-founder Jawed Karim (channel “jawed”), filmed at the San Diego Zoo in front of elephants. He remarks that they have “really, really, really long trunks.” URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNQXAC9IVRw. Listed duration 19 seconds; recent public counts about 443 million views and 20 million likes.  \n   Source: YouTube video page.\n\n2. **Upload date** — Commonly given as April 23, 2005 (some displays show April 24 depending on timezone). One detailed account places the upload at 8:27 p.m. UTC and says it was recorded by Yakov Lapitsky.  \n   Source: YouTube video metadata and on-page description summary.\n\n3. **What he says** — “All right, so here we are in front of the elephants. The cool thing about these guys is that they have really, really, really long trunks, and that’s cool. And that’s pretty much all there is to say.”  \n   Source: YouTube transcript.\n\n4. **Why it matters** — Karim, with co-founders Chad Hurley and Steve Chen, used the clip to show simple public video sharing. YouTube was sold to Google about 18 months later for stock then valued at roughly $1.65 billion.  \n   Source: ABC 10 News / KVUE anniversary segments (2025).\n\n5. **Location note** — San Diego Zoo has publicly said it is honored the first YouTube video was filmed there and marked the 20th anniversary in 2025.  \n   Source: San Diego Zoo comment and “Us at the Zoo” tribute video.\n\n6. **Background context** — Explanations of the clip often note Karim’s interest in easy video sharing (including difficulty finding the 2004 Janet Jackson Super Bowl clip online) and treat the zoo video as the public starting point of the platform.  \n   Source: EmpLemon, “Me at the zoo | YouTube Geographic” (2017).\n\n7. **“Not the first” claims** — Videos arguing an earlier upload (for example “comtest” or glitched 1970 dates) are generally treated as incorrect or based on timestamp bugs; upload capability is described as going live in April 2005, with this clip as the earliest public video.  \n   Source: Debunk and claim videos on YouTube (2020).\n\n8. **Ongoing interest** — View-count histories and 20-year anniversary coverage (2025) treat it as YouTube’s oldest surviving public upload and a frequent recreation spot at the zoo.  \n   Source: IvanIO view-history video; local news and zoo tributes, April 2025.",
